VIVARIUM V-4b-4 (docs/VIVARIUM.md section 6.11) -- readlink() on a system
with no symlinks.

Thylacine has NO symlink surface: there is no SYS_READLINK, and no EL0 path
creates or observes one. The 0001 seam therefore parked __NR_readlinkat at
the 0xFFFF ENOSYS sentinel, which is the wrong answer rather than an absent
one. On a symlink-free system the result of readlink() is KNOWN for every
path and POSIX already has the words: a path that exists and is not a
symlink is EINVAL, and a path that does not exist is that path's own error.

WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. musl's realpath() is a pure userspace
resolver (1.2.x; it does NOT use /proc/self/fd) that calls readlink() on
each path prefix and reads the errno as a fork in the road:

    if (k<0) { if (errno != EINVAL) return 0; ... continue ... }

EINVAL means "not a link, keep walking"; any other errno is fatal. Under
the ENOSYS sentinel realpath() therefore failed on its FIRST component --
so realpath() was broken for every path on the system, for every ported
program. That is a good deal larger than the /proc/self/exe gap that
surfaced it (task #66), and the truthful EINVAL repairs it whole with no
realpath patch at all: on a symlink-free system musl's resolver degenerates
into exactly what realpath should do there, which is canonicalize . / .. /
duplicate slashes and verify that every component exists.

THE /proc ARM. Linux presents four files as SYMLINKS that Thylacine
presents as REGULAR FILES whose contents are the target path:
/proc/{self,<pid>}/{exe,cwd}. Both kernel renders are deliberately bare
bytes -- no NUL, no newline -- and kernel/devproc.c says so at format_exe
and format_cwd, naming this consumer by name. So for those four shapes
readlink is an open + read and the byte count is the return value: the
information is identical and only the shape differs, which is precisely
what a boundary-line is for.

It is NOT a licence to open-and-read arbitrary paths -- that would turn
readlink() into a file-contents oracle. The match is a closed whitelist,
and a MISS is fail-safe rather than a wrong answer: an unmatched path falls
to the general arm, whose answer for a regular file is EINVAL, which is
literally true of every file we serve.

`self` is REWRITTEN to the caller's own pid rather than passed through.
Native /proc has no `self` entry at all (devproc's per-pid dirs are
numeric), so a pass-through would resolve only under a mounted diorama; and
the diorama's `self` resolves to the 9P connection's PEER -- the MOUNTER --
so under a SHARED mount it names the wrong Proc (VIVARIUM section 6.6). A
process knows its own pid, and saying so is both more portable and strictly
more correct. The arm is reachable at all only because V-4b-3 gave both
servers numeric per-pid directories; before it, `self` was the only
spelling either server understood.

Mechanism notes:
  - readlink() must delegate to readlinkat(). Stock musl issues the raw
    SYS_readlinkat syscall in readlink.c, so leaving that file alone would
    route the very caller that matters (realpath) straight past this
    implementation and back into the ENOSYS sentinel.
  - AT_FDCWD only, matching openat()'s posture -- a real dirfd is ENOTSUP
    rather than a guess. Relative paths still resolve, via the kernel's
    cwd-join (LS-4), which is the form realpath() actually uses.
  - An EMPTY exe/cwd render (kproc, a blob-loaded init, or an I-33
    fail-soft Path alloc failure) returns -ENOENT, not 0. realpath() reads
    a 0 return as ENOENT anyway, and a direct caller would otherwise take
    "" for a real link target.
  - Truncation follows POSIX: copy min(n, bufsize) and never NUL-terminate.

