+/* pouch boundary-line: readlink() / readlinkat() on a system with no symlinks
+ * (VIVARIUM V-4b-4; docs/VIVARIUM.md section 6.11).
+ *
+ * Thylacine has NO symlink surface at all -- there is no SYS_READLINK, and no
+ * EL0 path creates or observes one. The seam therefore parked __NR_readlinkat
+ * at the 0xFFFF ENOSYS sentinel. That was the wrong answer, and not merely an
+ * unimplemented one: on a system with no symlinks the result of readlink() is
+ * KNOWN for every path, and POSIX already has the word for it. A path that
+ * exists and is not a symlink is EINVAL; a path that does not exist is that
+ * path's own error.
+ *
+ * The distinction is load-bearing rather than pedantic, because musl's
+ * realpath() is a pure userspace resolver that calls readlink() on each path
+ * prefix and reads the errno as a fork in the road:
+ *
+ *     if (k<0) { if (errno != EINVAL) return 0; ... continue ... }
+ *
+ * EINVAL means "not a link, keep walking"; ANY other errno is fatal. Under the
+ * ENOSYS sentinel realpath() therefore failed on its very first component --
+ * so realpath() was broken for every path on the system, for every ported
+ * program, which is a good deal larger than the /proc/self/exe gap that
+ * surfaced it. The truthful EINVAL repairs it whole, with no realpath patch: on
+ * a symlink-free system musl's resolver degenerates into exactly what realpath
+ * should do there, which is canonicalize . / .. / duplicate slashes and verify
+ * that every component exists.
+ *
+ * THE /proc ARM (the phenotype half). Linux presents four files as SYMLINKS
+ * that Thylacine presents as REGULAR FILES whose contents are the target path:
+ * /proc/{self,<pid>}/{exe,cwd}. Both kernel renders are deliberately bare bytes
+ * -- no NUL, no newline -- and kernel/devproc.c says so at format_exe and
+ * format_cwd, naming this consumer. So for those paths readlink is an open +
+ * read and the byte count is the return value. This is the boundary-line doing
+ * its one job: the information is identical, only the shape differs, and
+ * translating shape is what this layer is for.
+ *
+ * It is NOT a licence to open-and-read arbitrary paths -- that would turn
+ * readlink() into a file-contents oracle. The match is a closed whitelist of
+ * four path shapes, and a MISS is fail-safe rather than a wrong answer: an
+ * unmatched path falls through to the general arm, whose answer for a regular
+ * file is EINVAL, which is literally true of every file we serve.
+ *
+ * `self` is REWRITTEN to our own pid rather than passed through. Native /proc
+ * has no `self` entry at all (devproc's per-pid dirs are numeric), so a
+ * pass-through would resolve only under a mounted diorama; and the diorama's
+ * `self` resolves to the 9P connection's PEER -- the MOUNTER -- so under a
+ * SHARED mount it names the wrong Proc (VIVARIUM section 6.6). A process knows
+ * its own pid; saying so is both more portable and strictly more correct.
